WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.245
This guy is named Slug. Slug created

00:00:04.245 --> 00:00:07.215
"Space War." If you go to the

00:00:07.215 --> 00:00:09.434
Computer History Museum on most weekends,

00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:11.624
Slug will come and kick your ass on the

00:00:11.624 --> 00:00:13.395
machine behind him which is the machine

00:00:13.395 --> 00:00:16.245
that Space War was invented on in 1951

00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:20.024
61 (my apologies) so you can go there (1961)

00:00:20.024 --> 00:00:21.915
and the guy who invented that computer

00:00:21.915 --> 00:00:25.694
history will come and kick your ass.

00:00:25.694 --> 00:00:27.435
This is very important because that is how

00:00:27.435 --> 00:00:30.075
constricted our online history is. Like

00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:31.785
the web is this little you know when I

00:00:31.785 --> 00:00:33.525
say the web is an eye blink in the

00:00:33.525 --> 00:00:36.255
history of humanity, right, it's like

00:00:36.255 --> 00:00:39.044
5,000 years from 'like oh my god a rock'

00:00:39.044 --> 00:00:41.715
to Kim Kardashian and then it's

00:00:41.715 --> 00:00:44.715
one millisecond to this, right?

00:00:44.715 --> 00:00:47.654
And also, for some people who care,

00:00:47.654 --> 00:00:49.485
there's no hover hand I touched him.

00:00:49.485 --> 00:00:52.515
I touched the man. I love him. Again, life

00:00:52.515 --> 00:00:54.945
is a lossy format, but software is not

00:00:54.945 --> 00:00:57.044
quite a lossy format. It likes to be

00:00:57.044 --> 00:00:59.655
completely together. And that's where

00:00:59.655 --> 00:01:01.304
luck comes in. So there's a thing I've

00:01:01.304 --> 00:01:02.444
been working on for about three years,

00:01:02.444 --> 00:01:04.035
this is what I started working on when

00:01:04.035 --> 00:01:05.925
Brewster hired me. One of things he said

00:01:05.925 --> 00:01:08.355
to me was: 'I would like for you---

00:01:08.355 --> 00:01:09.615
we've been doing pretty good with the web,

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:11.115
we've been okay with the web. We've been

00:01:11.115 --> 00:01:12.345
pretty good with books. We're actually

00:01:12.345 --> 00:01:13.785
doing good with music (we have a lot of

00:01:13.785 --> 00:01:16.304
music). We have a whole lot of movies.

00:01:16.304 --> 00:01:17.925
But what about software? How do we do things

00:01:17.925 --> 00:01:20.115
with software?' And the reason that movies

00:01:20.115 --> 00:01:22.124
and music persist is because we can play them.

00:01:22.124 --> 00:01:23.925
And not only can we play them,

00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:25.475
but we play them like nothing. Like if

00:01:25.475 --> 00:01:27.655
I'm like "Oh god listen to how terrible this band is."

00:01:27.835 --> 00:01:30.395
The time from "Man, what are you

00:01:30.395 --> 00:01:32.195
talking about?" to "You're right they suck!"

00:01:32.205 --> 00:01:35.015
can be like five seconds. That's amazing!

00:01:35.015 --> 00:01:36.975
Isn't that amazing? Like you forget,

00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:39.175
it's like "Oh my God, look what this cat is doing

00:01:39.355 --> 00:01:41.555
on top of... riding this Roomba... boom!

00:01:41.565 --> 00:01:43.154
You're looking at cat on Roomba!

00:01:43.155 --> 00:01:44.655
The world's a little dumber but you did it.

00:01:44.655 --> 00:01:47.995
2 seconds. So why don't we have that with software?

00:01:48.175 --> 00:01:51.935
This is the moment when my mind got blown!

00:01:52.095 --> 00:01:54.915
This is a game called "Smurf Rescue"

00:01:54.915 --> 00:01:56.715
from ColecoVision. Let's go back to it.

00:01:56.715 --> 00:01:59.975
In it you pilot a smurf. He has to jump

00:01:59.975 --> 00:02:01.895
over those things and meet Smurfette.

00:02:01.895 --> 00:02:03.635
Very simple. For the ColecoVision.

00:02:03.995 --> 00:02:05.415
But over the course of two and a half years

00:02:05.415 --> 00:02:08.215
we got it to run in a browser in JavaScript.

00:02:08.215 --> 00:02:10.915
And here's the thing about that...

00:02:10.915 --> 00:02:12.145
First of all 'Why would you do that

00:02:12.145 --> 00:02:14.065
abomination against all of man and Lord and...!'

00:02:14.065 --> 00:02:16.525
Okay, so the thing is is that

00:02:16.525 --> 00:02:19.285
right now the way to get to everybody is

00:02:19.285 --> 00:02:21.085
the web. The way that we get there is

00:02:21.085 --> 00:02:22.855
using a browser. And the thing in browsers that

00:02:22.855 --> 00:02:27.985
has not been destroyed by Larry Ellison is JavaScript.

00:02:27.985 --> 00:02:30.595
JavaScript is in everything (like it or not)

00:02:30.595 --> 00:02:33.155
and technology has been done to turn

00:02:33.355 --> 00:02:35.355
JavaScript into the end run for a compiler.

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:37.455
Without going into the full technical details,

00:02:37.455 --> 00:02:39.115
because they're all up there, the point is

00:02:39.175 --> 00:02:43.035
we were able to take a emulator called MESS, the

00:02:43.115 --> 00:02:46.055
Multi Emulator Super System, that has 1600 platforms

00:02:46.375 --> 00:02:48.675
everything from Sega Genesis

00:02:48.675 --> 00:02:51.535
to (Atari) 2600 to the ColecoVision

00:02:51.615 --> 00:02:53.415
and be able to make it play stuff

00:02:53.415 --> 00:02:55.615
in your browser basically in full speed.

00:02:55.615 --> 00:02:57.025
The first time we got it running it ran at

00:02:57.025 --> 00:02:58.675
14% speed. We had it in

00:02:58.675 --> 00:03:00.595
70% speed within two weeks.

00:03:00.595 --> 00:03:03.205
We had 24 systems running at

00:03:03.205 --> 00:03:05.485
100%  speed, and it's gone on like that.

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:07.855
We have been able to do all

00:03:07.855 --> 00:03:10.795
these different systems. We have old

00:03:10.795 --> 00:03:12.805
stuff from Atari. All these are running

00:03:12.805 --> 00:03:16.465
in JavaScript. Here's a chess system from

00:03:16.465 --> 00:03:20.095
01979. Here's "Spy Versus Spy". We've even

00:03:20.095 --> 00:03:21.805
gotten to the point--so, you know that's

00:03:21.805 --> 00:03:23.755
that's neat... and it's up there, if you go

00:03:23.755 --> 00:03:25.675
to archive.org, you go to /software...

00:03:25.675 --> 00:03:26.995
Boom! You could say to somebody:

00:03:26.995 --> 00:03:28.255
"I can't believe someone made a game about this.

00:03:28.255 --> 00:03:31.655
There it is!" Okay. So...

00:03:31.975 --> 00:03:35.315
from there it gets really weird and scary.

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:38.595
Because now once we've been able to make a machine

00:03:38.595 --> 00:03:40.455
run in a machine, where to go from there?

00:03:40.495 --> 00:03:42.265
Well, we have something I call...

00:03:42.265 --> 00:03:44.455
"The Emularity." This is windows running

00:03:44.455 --> 00:03:48.235
inside of a browser. And we actually have

00:03:48.235 --> 00:03:51.175
it booting up... so that's JavaScript.

00:03:51.175 --> 00:03:53.785
And we've actually gotten PPP to work,

00:03:53.785 --> 00:03:55.945
so that the browser can connect through PPP...

00:03:55.945 --> 00:03:59.065
out of the browser into the Internet.

00:03:59.065 --> 00:04:01.795
So it's connecting to textfiles.com using

00:04:01.795 --> 00:04:06.205
the Mosaic browser... and

00:04:06.205 --> 00:04:10.165
is able to connect... so it's a 01994 browser

00:04:10.165 --> 00:04:12.804
inside of a 01993 operating system that's

00:04:12.804 --> 00:04:15.775
running inside of a 02015 browser that's

00:04:15.775 --> 00:04:21.775
running inside of a 02013 operating system. Right?

00:04:21.775 --> 00:04:26.544
We're here now! Now just to be clear

00:04:26.544 --> 00:04:28.405
the current Internet does not like you...

00:04:28.405 --> 00:04:33.025
Whitehouse.gov is like... what are you?

00:04:33.025 --> 00:04:36.324
Forsooth! I cometh with the Magna Carta,

00:04:36.324 --> 00:04:39.174
to bringeth to you... but that's what

00:04:39.174 --> 00:04:40.674
we... okay, fine, I understand that...

00:04:40.674 --> 00:04:42.745
but the best part is it actually

00:04:42.745 --> 00:04:44.245
leaves something in the log so it'll

00:04:44.245 --> 00:04:46.315
actually say... well, 16-bit Windows 3.1

00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:48.535
Netscape 1.0 stopped by...

00:04:49.275 --> 00:04:53.975
that'll blow your metrics right the heck out!

00:05:01.015 --> 00:05:03.075
 

